{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfu1nSax94fCqzSHP68n5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Fix Step 3 first.**\n\nAsking someone to log in before they can reset a forgotten password is a dead end. Anyone who follows the link is stuck, and the only way out is a call to support. That makes it the most likely cause of the reset calls, though I haven't seen any data to confirm it.\n\n**What to do this sprint**\n1. **Step 3 (core of the sprint):** Make the emailed link go straight to a \"set new password\" screen, with no login. Use a single-use token that expires quickly, such as 15–30 minutes. If app deep links (universal or app links) won't fit in the sprint, ship the mobile-web reset page first. Opening the app can come later. Check that the login requirement isn't a deliberate security control. If it is, replace it with a step like an emailed code, because a login can't work here.\n2. **Step 4 (small, ship alongside):** Show the rules before the user types, as a live checklist that ticks off as they go. Name the specific rule that failed, including \"can't match your last five passwords\". This is mostly front-end work and cuts retries.\n\n**Leave alone or defer**\n- **Step 2:** The wording is correct. It doesn't reveal whether an account exists, so keep it.\n- **Step 1:** Moving the link up or making it more visible is a quick fix. Do it only if there's slack, because it affects discovery rather than completion.\n\n**Measure:** Track the reset completion rate (link opened to password set) and reset-related call volume, before and after. Also ask support for the breakdown of reset calls by cause. If most of them are about \"can't get in from the link\", that confirms the priority."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3447,"cache_read_input_tokens":5101,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_toke